# Loom projects

A single loom file is enough to write a small program. For real-life scenarios, Loom supports whole projects and modules as a literate corpus: a folder of loom files that are read together as a book.

```bash
loom init
```

## Initialise project

To set up a project, run the `loom init` command. Loom asks which language your sections use by default. It's TypeScript unless you name another, so press Enter.

Your answer goes into `.loom/config.yaml`, the project's settings. Commit it. Beside it, `.loom/.gitignore` keeps out the language servers Loom downloads. The folder that holds `.loom` is now a Loom project.

Running `loom init` again changes nothing, and it never touches the looms you already have.

## A home for your looms

Looms live in a folder called `corpus`, and `loom init` creates it for you. Move your loom in:

```bash
mv hello.loom corpus/
```

A loom in `corpus` writes its files to the project folder, the one that holds `corpus`. So `hello.ts` still lands where it did.

## One label fewer

The project now has a primary language, TypeScript, and every section that names no language uses it. Open `corpus/hello.loom` and take `{TypeScript}` off the first heading, so it reads `# Saying hello`.

## Tangle the project

Point `loom tangle` at the folder rather than at one file:

```bash
loom tangle corpus
```

Loom reads every loom in `corpus` and writes `hello.ts`, the same file as before. It also records what it wrote in `.loom/loom.lock`, so it can tell its own files from yours. Commit that too.
